  "slug": "subarray-sums-divisible-by-k",
  "title": "Subarray Sums Divisible by K",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Prefix sum",
  "patterns": [
    "prefix sum",
    "hash map"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "a subarray's sum is divisible by k exactly when its two boundary prefix sums have the SAME remainder mod k - why?",
    "because (prefixSum[j] - prefixSum[i]) mod k == 0 exactly when prefixSum[j] mod k == prefixSum[i] mod k",
    "keep a hash map counting how many times each remainder has appeared among prefix sums so far (seed remainder 0 with count 1, for the empty prefix)",
    "at each step, compute the current remainder, add however many times that remainder has been seen before to your answer, then increment its count"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "not handling negative sums correctly when computing the remainder (in some languages, negative mod needs normalizing into [0, k))",
    "forgetting to seed the remainder map with 0 occurring once before any elements are processed",
    "confusing 'count of subarrays with sum divisible by k' with 'longest subarray divisible by k', which needs first-occurrence indices instead of counts"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of integers and an integer `k`.\n\n**Return the number of contiguous subarrays whose sum is divisible by k.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 3 * 10^4",
      "-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4",
      "1 <= k <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "no subarray is divisible by k",
      "the entire array itself is divisible by k",
      "an array containing a zero (a subarray of just that zero is always divisible)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
